<body>

<header class="masthead">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="kicker">Hearing Benefit Services · Process walkthrough for team review</p>
    <h1>Federal Retiree ($0 Program):<br>how a lead moves from form to fitting</h1>
    <p>This is the proposed process, not a built system. Read it, mark anything
       that doesn't match how you actually work, and we'll adjust before a single
       workflow gets created in GoHighLevel.</p>
    <p class="stamp">Draft for review — nothing has been built yet</p>
  </div>
</header>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Why this pipeline is different</h2>
    <p class="lede">Every other HBS pipeline tracks a sale. This one tracks an
      insurance approval that happens <em>before</em> a sale is possible.</p>
    <p>A federal retiree who fills out the qualification form has not bought
      anything and can't be quoted yet. Their FEHB plan decides what happens next.
      So the first half of this pipeline is verification work, and the second half
      only starts once we know what their benefit actually covers.</p>
    <p>Once a patient is approved, this pipeline is finished. They hand off to the
      Online Store Purchase pipeline, which already handles ordering, programming,
      shipping, and follow-up. We are not rebuilding any of that.</p>

    <div class="legend">
      <div class="l-auto">
        <strong>Green — the system does it</strong>
        <p>Fires on its own. Nobody has to remember anything or click anything.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="l-person">
        <strong>Purple — a hearing professional does it</strong>
        <p>Requires a person to check something and then apply a tag. The tag is
           what starts the next automation.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>What we're reusing instead of rebuilding</h2>
    <p class="lede">Four systems already exist and already work. This pipeline
      plugs into them.</p>
    <ul class="keeps">
      <li><b>Appointment reminders.</b> The 24-hour, 3-hour and 15-minute
        reminders, plus cancellation and reschedule handling, already run on every
        booking. Federal consults use the same calendar and the same reminders.</li>
      <li><b>Order fulfillment.</b> Ordering from Signia, programming, shipping,
        the Day 2 check-in, the two-week follow-up and the review request are all
        in the Online Store Purchase pipeline. Approved patients get moved there.</li>
      <li><b>STOP opt-outs.</b> Anyone who replies STOP is already removed from
        messaging account-wide. No separate federal version needed.</li>
      <li><b>Email bounce protection.</b> Emails only send to addresses that are
        present and haven't previously bounced, so this campaign can't damage
        sending reputation.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>The ten stages</h2>
    <p class="lede">A lead sits in exactly one stage at a time. Every message
      below is draft copy — change the wording freely.</p>

    <div class="stage is-auto">
      <div class="rail"><span class="num">1</span><span class="chip chip-auto">Automated</span></div>
      <div>
        <h3>New lead — form submitted</h3>
        <p>The "See If I Qualify" form is submitted from the federal retiree page.
          Everything here happens within seconds.</p>
        <ul class="does">
          <li>Contact enters the pipeline at this stage</li>
          <li>Tagged as a federal benefits lead</li>
          <li>Text and email confirmation go out</li>
          <li>A verification task is created for the hearing professional</li>
          <li>Lead advances automatically to stage 2</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="sms">
          <span>Text sent to the patient</span>
          <q>Hi {{contact.first_name}}, this is Hearing Benefit Services. We got
            your request to check your federal hearing benefits. We'll review your
            plan and let you know exactly what it covers. Reply STOP to opt out.</q>
        </div>
        <p class="tagline">Tag applied: <code>fed_retiree_lead</code></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stage is-person">
      <div class="rail"><span class="num">2</span><span class="chip chip-person">Hearing professional</span></div>
      <div>
        <h3>Insurance verification</h3>
        <p>This is the stage that decides everything downstream. FEHB benefits vary
          by carrier, by plan tier, and by plan year, so this can't be automated.</p>
        <ul class="does">
          <li>Identify the carrier — BCBS, GEHA, Aetna, UHC, or another</li>
          <li>Confirm whether the plan includes a hearing aid benefit</li>
          <li>Check the dollar cap and how often it resets</li>
          <li>Check whether an in-network hearing test is required first</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Then apply one tag. That tag decides which of the next three stages the
          patient lands in, and sends the matching message.</p>
        <p class="tagline">Tags: <code>fed_qualified_zero</code> ·
          <code>fed_needs_network_test</code> · <code>fed_needs_more_info</code></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stage is-auto">
      <div class="rail"><span class="num">3</span><span class="chip chip-auto">Automated</span></div>
      <div>
        <h3>Needs more information</h3>
        <p>We couldn't verify the plan from what the patient gave us on the form.</p>
        <div class="sms">
          <span>Text sent to the patient</span>
          <q>Hi {{contact.first_name}}, to check your hearing benefit we need a
            little more from your plan card: the carrier name, plan name, and your
            member ID. You can reply here with a photo of the card.</q>
        </div>
        <p>A follow-up task is created. Once the details come in, the hearing
          professional returns the patient to stage 2 and verifies again.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stage is-auto">
      <div class="rail"><span class="num">4</span><span class="chip chip-auto">Automated</span></div>
      <div>
        <h3>Needs in-network hearing test</h3>
        <p>The plan requires a test from an in-network provider before it will pay.
          We send the patient out, but we don't lose them.</p>
        <div class="sms">
          <span>Text sent to the patient</span>
          <q>Hi {{contact.first_name}}, your plan asks for a hearing test from an
            in-network provider before benefits apply. Once you have those results,
            send them to us and we'll take it from there.</q>
        </div>
        <p>A check-back reminder fires automatically after 14 days so nobody has to
          keep a mental list of who was sent out for testing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stage is-auto">
      <div class="rail"><span class="num">5</span><span class="chip chip-auto">Automated</span></div>
      <div>
        <h3>Qualified — consultation needed</h3>
        <p>The benefit is confirmed. Now we book.</p>
        <div class="sms">
          <span>Text sent to the patient</span>
          <q>Good news {{contact.first_name}} — we've reviewed your plan and your
            hearing benefit is active. The next step is a short consultation with
            our specialist. Book a time here:
            hearingbenefitservices.com/appointment</q>
        </div>
        <p>If they don't book, follow-up messages continue on a set schedule rather
          than relying on someone remembering to chase.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stage is-auto">
      <div class="rail"><span class="num">6</span><span class="chip chip-auto">Automated</span></div>
      <div>
        <h3>Consultation booked</h3>
        <p>Booking the appointment moves the patient here on its own. The existing
          confirmation and reminder sequence takes over from this point — no
          separate federal reminders, no duplicate texts.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stage is-auto">
      <div class="rail"><span class="num">7</span><span class="chip chip-auto">Automated</span></div>
      <div>
        <h3>Consultation completed</h3>
        <p>When the appointment is marked complete, the patient moves here and a
          task appears asking for the outcome. Two outcomes are possible, and the
          hearing professional chooses one at stage 8 or stage 9.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stage is-person">
      <div class="rail"><span class="num">8</span><span class="chip chip-person">Hearing professional</span></div>
      <div>
        <h3>Approved — $0 program</h3>
        <p>Benefits cover the devices in full. This is the win.</p>
        <ul class="does">
          <li>The federal opportunity is marked won and closes here</li>
          <li>A new order automatically opens in the Online Store Purchase pipeline</li>
          <li>Fulfillment picks it up from "Hearing Aid Ordered on Signia"</li>
        </ul>
        <p>From this point the patient is handled by the existing fulfillment
          process. Nothing about ordering, programming or shipping changes.</p>
        <p class="tagline">Tags: <code>fed_approved_zero</code> ·
          <code>fed_converted_zero</code></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stage is-person">
      <div class="rail"><span class="num">9</span><span class="chip chip-person">Alternative option</span></div>
      <div>
        <h3>Rental or private pay</h3>
        <p>The patient wants to move forward but benefits won't cover it — out of
          network, benefit exhausted, or only partial coverage.</p>
        <div class="sms">
          <span>Text sent to the patient</span>
          <q>Hi {{contact.first_name}}, your plan won't cover the full cost, but
            you still have good options. We can walk you through a monthly plan or
            our rental program — no large payment up front.</q>
        </div>
        <p>Financing details and the Cherry application link are included in the
          follow-up email so the patient can apply on their own time.</p>
        <p class="tagline">Tags: <code>fed_alt_option</code> ·
          <code>fed_converted_paid</code> if they buy</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stage is-auto">
      <div class="rail"><span class="num">10</span><span class="chip chip-auto">Automated</span></div>
      <div>
        <h3>Closed lost — long-term nurture</h3>
        <p>No benefit, no response, or price was the blocker. These contacts aren't
          deleted. They move into a slow educational sequence and stay reachable
          for the next plan year, when their coverage may have changed.</p>
        <p class="tagline">Tag: <code>fed_nurture</code></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Tags at a glance</h2>
    <p class="lede">Tags are the switches. Applying one starts the matching
      automation; they also stay on the record permanently as history.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Tag</th><th>Applied by</th><th>What it starts</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>fed_retiree_lead</code></td><td>System</td><td>Marks the contact as a federal benefits lead</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>fed_qualified_zero</code></td><td>Hearing professional</td><td>Sends the good-news text and booking link</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>fed_needs_network_test</code></td><td>Hearing professional</td><td>Sends the in-network instructions and sets a 14-day check-back</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>fed_needs_more_info</code></td><td>Hearing professional</td><td>Requests plan card details</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>fed_approved_zero</code></td><td>Hearing professional</td><td>Closes the file as won and opens the fulfillment order</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>fed_alt_option</code></td><td>Hearing professional</td><td>Sends rental and financing options</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>fed_converted_zero</code></td><td>System</td><td>Records a covered-by-benefits sale</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>fed_converted_paid</code></td><td>Hearing professional</td><td>Records a self-pay or rental sale</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>fed_nurture</code></td><td>System</td><td>Moves the contact into long-term follow-up</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Language we need to be careful with</h2>
    <div class="note">
      <h3>Nothing should promise "$0" before verification</h3>
      <p>FEHB hearing aid coverage is not uniform. Some plans pay in full, some
        cap the benefit at a fixed dollar amount every few years, and some require
        an in-network provider before anything is paid at all.</p>
      <p>Any message sent before we've checked the plan should say we're
        <em>checking whether the plan covers it</em> — not that the patient
        qualifies for free hearing aids. Once the benefit is confirmed at stage 2,
        we can be as specific as the plan allows.</p>
      <p>The same applies to the landing page headline, since that's what sets the
        expectation before the form is even submitted. Worth a read-through before
        this campaign goes live.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Decisions needed before we build</h2>
    <p class="lede">These are the open items. Each one changes how the build is
      configured, so we need answers first rather than reworking later.</p>
    <ol class="decisions">
      <li><b>Who verifies benefits?</b>
        <span>One person owning verification, or shared? Whoever owns it receives
          every stage 2 task.</span></li>
      <li><b>Same calendar or a federal-specific one?</b>
        <span>A separate calendar gives cleaner reporting on federal consults.
          The same calendar is simpler and reuses the existing reminders.</span></li>
      <li><b>What are we recording about the plan?</b>
        <span>We should store carrier, plan name and member ID on the contact so
          verification isn't repeated from scratch on every follow-up.</span></li>
      <li><b>Are the rental terms confirmed?</b>
        <span>The $250/month figure came from an outside draft. Confirm the real
          terms before it goes into any message.</span></li>
      <li><b>How long before a non-responder is closed?</b>
        <span>How many follow-up attempts at each stage, and over how many days,
          before the lead moves to long-term nurture?</span></li>
      <li><b>Does the in-network referral get tracked?</b>
        <span>Patients sent out for testing are a real source of return business.
          The 14-day check-back covers it — confirm that's the right interval.</span></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<footer>
  <div class="wrap">
    <p>Hearing Benefit Services · Honolulu, HI · Internal process document.
      This describes a proposed pipeline that has not yet been built. Send
      corrections before the build begins.</p>
  </div>
</footer>

